WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.639
Context-aware XSS prevention starts by
tracing untrusted data to the browser

2
00:00:04.719 --> 00:00:06.538
operation that will interpret it.

3
00:00:06.618 --> 00:00:09.503
For ordinary text, use textContent,
createTextNode,

4
00:00:09.583 --> 00:00:11.527
or normal framework interpolation.

5
00:00:11.607 --> 00:00:14.116
For structure, create elements or
components.

6
00:00:14.196 --> 00:00:18.460
These APIs preserve the distinction
between developer-authored structure and

7
00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:22.868
external data, avoiding source-code
construction instead of trying to repair it

8
00:00:22.948 --> 00:00:23.575
afterward.

9
00:00:23.655 --> 00:00:26.916
When a system must generate source, name
the precise context.

10
00:00:26.996 --> 00:00:30.759
HTML text and quoted attributes need their
appropriate HTML handling.

11
00:00:30.839 --> 00:00:33.285
URL components need URL-aware
construction,

12
00:00:33.365 --> 00:00:37.943
while URL-bearing properties also require
protocol, origin, or destination policy.

13
00:00:38.023 --> 00:00:42.288
Avoid placing external strings into
JavaScript, CSS, or event-handler source;

14
00:00:42.368 --> 00:00:45.880
prefer structured data, fixed handlers,
controlled properties,

15
00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:47.277
and allowlisted values.

16
00:00:47.357 --> 00:00:50.806
Input validation decides whether data fits
the business domain.

17
00:00:50.886 --> 00:00:55.151
Output handling decides how accepted data
is represented safely at a particular

18
00:00:55.231 --> 00:00:55.544
sink.

19
00:00:55.624 --> 00:00:58.258
When rich HTML is an intentional product
feature,

20
00:00:58.338 --> 00:01:01.725
use a maintained parsing-based sanitizer
with a narrow policy.

21
00:01:01.805 --> 00:01:05.254
Protect the result from later unsafe
concatenation or decoding.

22
00:01:05.334 --> 00:01:09.473
Framework autoescaping and structured
rendering should remain the default,

23
00:01:09.553 --> 00:01:13.504
while raw-HTML APIs, trust bypasses,
disabled escaping, embedded state,

24
00:01:13.584 --> 00:01:17.724
direct DOM manipulation, and third-party
renderers receive focused review.

25
00:01:17.804 --> 00:01:22.131
TypeScript types can make the boundary
visible but cannot perform runtime safety

26
00:01:22.211 --> 00:01:23.214
transformations.

27
00:01:23.294 --> 00:01:27.622
Finally, verify the design across every
source-to-sink path and add containment

28
00:01:27.702 --> 00:01:28.141
layers.

29
00:01:28.221 --> 00:01:32.046
Regression tests should cover server and
client rendering, URL policy,

30
00:01:32.126 --> 00:01:34.133
sanitization, and post-processing.

31
00:01:34.213 --> 00:01:38.541
CSP can restrict active content, Trusted
Types can gate covered injection sinks,

32
00:01:38.621 --> 00:01:41.945
and HttpOnly can protect selected cookies
from script access.

33
00:01:42.025 --> 00:01:44.220
None replaces correct context handling.

34
00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:46.871
Do not ask only whether a string has been
escaped.

35
00:01:46.951 --> 00:01:49.021
Ask what will interpret the value next,

36
00:01:49.101 --> 00:01:51.860
and use the defense designed for that
exact context.
